In this Continual-ARC instance, eight demonstrations varied dramatically in dimensions (from 10x6 to 29x30), palette, background, edge-bar placement, sparse marker locations, and output deltas. Some demos extended a line from a marker, others filled regions, and others changed differently shaped areas. The test was a 14x11 grid with a 3-cell color-2 bar at the top and bottom, plus three isolated color-3 cells.

The outputs did not support a single obvious selector/operation by visual analogy. Despite that, multiple unsupported candidates were submitted: extend a marker-to-edge ray, replace marker color, recolor bars, stamp/paint motif regions, and fill regions. They were all rejected. The reusable lesson is to maintain an exact delta ledger and require a deterministic rule that replays every pair before transfer. Extreme demo heterogeneity and repeated wrong feedback should increase caution, not prompt cosmetic variants. Do not assume that markers become color 4 or that edge bars imply rays; those features appeared in distinct, incompatible demos. Following wrong feedback, retire the entire candidate and reassess task class/output mapping. When no rule can be validated, avoid cycling through speculative guesses; honor action protocol while being explicit internally about uncertainty.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-4/play/users/u043/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/task-36d8805c-review.md"}

